  "body": "Follow-up of #4391. AGENTS.md \"Commands ship with an MCP tool\".\n\n## What happens\n\nThe MCP tool `edit_user_holder` (#4391) is live. The generated reference `docs/agents/mcp-tools.md` and the user guide name it. The `camcraft-mcp` skill has no row for it. The skill is a process file, so the PR for #4391 does not change it.\n\n## Expected\n\nAdd this row to `.claude/skills/camcraft-mcp/SKILL.md`, after the `upsert_tool_component` row:\n\n```\n- `edit_user_holder { \"id\": string, \"name\"?: string, \"taper\"?: string, \"clamp\"?: \"ER11\"..\"ER50\" | \"weldon\" | \"shrink\", \"bore\"?: mm, \"gauge_len\"?: mm, \"nose_d\"?: mm, \"largest_d\"?: mm }`: Edit a holder of the document table by its shape, as the holder form does (`HolderShape` outline, one `EditDocumentComponent`). An unstated field keeps the holder's value; an ER or Weldon clamp without `largest_d` takes `nose_d`. Every tool built from the holder is derived again in the same undo step. Returns `{ id, name, shape, updated_tools }`. Refused by name: an id the document does not hold, a holder whose outline is not a shape outline (use `upsert_tool_component`), an unknown taper or clamp, a value that cannot describe steel (the `ShapeError` text), an empty name, an edit that changes nothing, an edit one of its tools cannot take (component and tool named; nothing changes).\n```\n\n## Done means\n\n- The row is in the skill. The PR body has the line `Process change: yes`.\n",
